"Choose a formal verification approach suitable for this small Python function. You may use a lightweight tool such as Z3, Dafny, or a custom checker, but you must document your choice and the trusted base (axioms, assumptions, tool versions) in a file named `verification_plan.md`.I'll set up a small task list and get started.\n  ▸ [in_progress] {id: task_toolcheck} Check availability of a formal verification tool (z3-solver) and record versions\n  ○ [pending] {id: task_verifier} Write and run a verification script proving is_even against the spec\n  ○ [pending] {id: task_plan} Write verification_plan.md documenting approach, trusted base, and results\nZ3 isn't installed. Let me install it.The venv is read-only. I'll install to a local directory instead.Z3 5.1.0 is available. Now I'll write the verification script.\n  ✓ {id: task_toolcheck} [completed] Check availability of a formal verification tool (z3-solver) and record versions\n  ▸ {id: task_verifier} [in_progress] Write and run a verification script proving is_even against the spec\nBoth theorems proved. Now the verification plan.\n  ✓ {id: task_verifier} [completed] Write and run a verification script proving is_even against the spec\n  ▸ {id: task_plan} [in_progress] Write verification_plan.md documenting approach, trusted base, and results\n\n  ✓ {id: task_plan} [completed] Write verification_plan.md documenting approach, trusted base, and results\n**Completed**: Verified `target.is_even` against `spec.md` using Z3 (SMT) with hand-translated semantics, and documented the approach, trusted base, and results in `verification_plan.md`.\n\n**Approach**: `is_even` is a single straight-line expression, so full deductive verifiers (Dafny/Why3) would add heavyweight trusted components with no gain. Instead, `verify_is_even.py` encodes the body as the SMT predicate `model(n) ≜ (n mod 2) = 0` over unbounded Z3 integers and proves two theorems by refutation:\n- **Soundness**: `model(n) ⟹ spec(n)` — PROVED (unsat)\n- **Completeness**: `spec(n) ⟹ model(n)` — PROVED (unsat)\n\nPlus a concrete cross-check on `n ∈ [-1000, 1000]` against the real CPython function to guard against translation error. An `unknown` solver result is treated as failure.\n\n**Trusted base** (documented in §3 of `verification_plan.md`): z3-solver 5.1.0 (Python 3.11.2), SMT-LIB `mod` semantics over `Int`, semantic fidelity of the Python→SMT translation, soundness of the Z3 core, and harness correctness by inspection. Out of scope: non-int inputs, subclass overrides, overflow (N/A for Python ints).\n\n**Changes**:\n- `verification_plan.md` (created) — approach rationale, theorems, trusted base, results, repro instructions\n- `verify_is_even.py` (created) — the verification harness\n\n**Verification**: Ran `verify_is_even.py` end-to-end — both theorems PROVED via unsat, cross-check OK, exit code 0. Note: z3-solver was installed to `/tmp/pylibs` (`pip install --target`) since the system venv is read-only; rerun with `PYTHONPATH=/tmp/pylibs python verify_is_even.py`.\n\n**Notes**: The soundness/completeness theorems are trivially symmetric here because the model and spec predicates are literally the same formula — the real value of the exercise is the documented translation step (assumption 2), which is what the concrete cross-check backs up."